                <pb facs="0111" n="26" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg26"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.00/1550: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gonzales</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-05-16T13:00:00-05:29"><hi rendition="#italic">May 16, 1919, 1
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received 3.35
                            p.m.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p>27. My May 11th, midnight. In a published address Rafael Montalvo who was
                    expected to be nominated for President by Conservative convention this month
                    announces that congressional committee is working with General Crowder on
                    electoral reform law which country needs and as an elemental courtesy to General
                    Crowder and Congress he advocates postponement of presidential nominations and
                    asks friends to cease efforts for early convening of convention of Conservative
                    Party.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gonzales</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
